  "summary": "Today's health-tech landscape shows several competing dynamics reshaping the industry. The Trump administration is moving aggressively on multiple fronts: advancing peptide drug production through specialty pharmacies—a priority for Health Secretary RFK Jr.—while simultaneously intensifying health care fraud investigations, with DOJ officials invoking Theranos as a cautionary tale for executives. On the innovation side, AI-driven biotech is attracting substantial capital, with Iambic Therapeutics raising $150 million in its IPO for AI-powered cancer drug development, while Merck and Moderna are advancing their partnership combining Keytruda with personalized mRNA cancer vaccines, with early results already showing patient benefits. Primary care access and home-based interventions are also gaining traction, evidenced by Rosarium Health's partnership with Anthem Medi-Cal for home modifications and increased focus on reimagining primary care around continuous engagement rather than episodic visits. Meanwhile, public health concerns are mounting, with measles cases surging—jeopardizing the U.S.'s disease-free status—and AI tools emerging to forecast outbreak hotspots, while binge-eating disorder among high school students affects roughly 40 percent according to new CDC data. Finally, former FDA commissioners from across administrations are publicly criticizing current HHS leadership, signaling tension within the regulatory establishment over the agency's direction and recent staff departures.",
